    "evidence_text": "Ben Johnston (composer)\n\nBenjamin Burwell Johnston, Jr. (born March 15, 1926 in Macon, Georgia) is a composer of contemporary music in just intonation: \"one of the foremost composers of microtonal music\" .\n\nPascale Criton (born 1954) is a French musicologist and a composer of contemporary music, more specifically microtonal music.\n\n She is particularly known for exploiting very dense microtonal scales such as 1/12 tone or 1/16 and beyond for the particular perception properties they imply.\n\n The Foundation presents frequent concerts (originally in Teylers Museum) presenting contemporary, early, popular, and improvised microtonal music.\n\n He also wrote extensively on the American experimental tradition, microtonal music and spectral music, including the work of such figures as James Tenney, Hora\u021biu R\u0103dulescu, Claude Vivier, and Frank Denyer.\n\n The most important works include his String quartets, which document and demonstrate the development of the composer's style (microtonal music) and his most innovative opera: \"Matka\" (Mother).\n\nNewband is a contemporary music ensemble devoted to the performance of microtonal music.",
